**Vitamin D — Sourced Evidence Summary**

According to the NHS, vitamin D helps regulate the amount of calcium and phosphate in the body, nutrients the NHS describes as needed to keep bones, teeth, and muscles healthy (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d). The NHS additionally states, regarding the colecalciferol form of vitamin D, that it helps the body absorb more calcium and phosphorus from food and helps the kidneys retain calcium (https://www.nhs.uk/medicines/colecalciferol/about-colecalciferol).

Food sources listed by the NHS include oily fish such as salmon, sardines, trout, herring, and mackerel, red meat, egg yolks, liver, and fortified foods such as some fat spreads and breakfast cereals. The NHS notes that cows' milk in the UK is generally not a good source of vitamin D as it is not fortified, and that a vegan diet contains little or no vitamin D without fortified foods or supplements (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d; http://www.nhs.uk/live-well/bone-health/food-for-strong-bones).

The UK reference nutrient intake, as stated by NICE, is 10 micrograms per day (equivalent to 400 IU) for adults and children aged 4 and over (https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ph56/chapter/glossary). The NHS states that adults, including pregnant and breastfeeding women and the elderly, and children aged 11 to 17, should not take more than 100 micrograms (4,000 IU) of vitamin D a day, as it could be harmful. The NHS also states that taking too many vitamin D supplements over a long period of time can cause too much calcium to build up in the body (https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/vitamins-and-minerals/vitamin-d).

